usb: gadget: ci_udc: guard handle_ep_complete() against an empty queue

udc_irq() decides which endpoints to service purely from the hardware
epcomp completion bitmap (ci_udc.c, the "for (i = 0; i < NUM_ENDPOINTS
&& n; i++)" loop). If a completion bit is set for an endpoint whose
software-side ci_ep->queue is already empty -- e.g. stop_activity()
ran during a disconnect/reset that raced with an in-flight transfer,
or the hardware raises a duplicate/stale completion for a transfer
already processed -- handle_ep_complete() is called anyway.

It then does:

	ci_req = list_first_entry(&ci_ep->queue, struct ci_req, queue);

list_first_entry() is documented in include/linux/list.h as requiring
a non-empty list. On an empty list it computes
container_of(&ci_ep->queue, struct ci_req, queue) -- a valid-looking
pointer, backward from ci_ep->queue itself (part of the static
controller.ep[] array), not NULL and not obviously wild. The result
is silent type confusion: ci_req->dtd_count, ->hw_buf etc. actually
read other fields of struct ci_ep (or adjacent memory) instead of a
real ci_req. Since ci_ep->queue's address and the surrounding struct
layout are fixed at link time, this misread is deterministic given
the same boot path -- which is why this reproduced with byte-for-byte
identical register dumps across unrelated boots.

The corrupted dtd_count then drives handle_ep_complete()'s
queue-item walk (ci_invalidate_td(next_td), next_td = item->next)
past any real descriptor chain into garbage, and free()s a bogus
pointer on the next iteration -- observed on hardware as
"CACHE: Misaligned operation at range [00000001, 00000041]" followed
by a data abort inside free(), called from udc_irq().

There's nothing to complete when the queue is empty, so just return.

--- a/drivers/usb/gadget/ci_udc.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/gadget/ci_udc.c
@@ -677,6 +677,22 @@
 	in = (ci_ep->desc->bEndpointAddress & USB_DIR_IN) != 0;
 	item = ci_get_qtd(num, in);
 	ci_invalidate_qtd(num);
+
+	/*
+	 * A hardware completion bit can fire for an endpoint whose software
+	 * queue was already emptied (e.g. by stop_activity() during a
+	 * disconnect/reset that raced with an in-flight transfer, or a
+	 * duplicate completion for a transfer already processed).
+	 * list_first_entry() is documented as unsafe on an empty list: it
+	 * computes container_of(&ci_ep->queue, struct ci_req, queue), which
+	 * silently reads ci_req fields from the wrong memory (here, other
+	 * fields of struct ci_ep) instead of crashing outright. That
+	 * misread dtd_count/hw_buf then drives an out-of-bounds queue-item
+	 * walk and a free() on a bogus pointer. Nothing to complete here.
+	 */
+	if (list_empty(&ci_ep->queue))
+		return;
+
 	ci_req = list_first_entry(&ci_ep->queue, struct ci_req, queue);
 
 	next_td = item;
